A||N||5||Shianne||84 2m4 engine||||1||
Z||000000||Shianne||05-15-2001||09:58 PM||||What can you do about an engine with significant blow by.  I am looking at building a higher performacne 2.5l, but am unsure of which block and parts are the best.  Or I am better using my existing motor, or is there some way of making it's blow by less.  Other than that the car runs great.  It only has about 100000 miles.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Vader||05-15-2001||10:16 PM||||Shianne,<P>You can significantly reduce blowby with a new set of rings (about $40.00).  Unfortunately, unless you have been using synthetic oil for most of the life of the engine, you are likely to encounter a significant ridge and bore taper, especially on the inner cylinders (2 & 3) requiring boring and new pistons.<P>As long as you are going that far, you might as well install larger valves and better springs once the head is off, and open the tops of the intake runners and short-side radii of the ports (both intake and exhaust) to maximize flow.  Of course, this will require enlarging the valve bowls and getting rid of that stupid swirl port "hump" in the top of the bowl, and will also require enlarging the intake manifold runners.  Enlarge teh intake and exhaust ports but retain the rounded corners of the exhaust ports.  You can throw away that little stamped plate under the throttle body and blend the well to the runners better, then install a larger injector and TB from a CFI engine.<P>Remove that worn out cam and gears and install a Comp 260H cam and new gears at about a 6° advance.  Polish and fillet the crank, check rods and balance, then go out and hunt down some V-6 'P' cars just for grins.<P>I'm sure that's what I would do:<P> <IMG SRC="http://homestead.juno.com/jlstrey/files/Porting.jpg"> <P>You wanted to build a stout 151, after all...<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Make Me Bad</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000002||Shianne||05-15-2001||10:31 PM||||So building a new engine sounds like the best thing to do, although I am not sure the car is worth it, it is in good shape.  But I only paid about 350.00 US for it.  Heck of a deal, It has most options but is the econline model.  Not all that fast.  But the best handling car of all the sports cars I have owened.  <P>I would like to build a engine that is in the 120 to 130 hp range.  but have read alot about low end problems and crank or bearing falure.  I have also heard of people using the crank from the s10 pickups.  Is this possible?  Or am I better stick with what is there.<P>I am likely going to by a core and rebuild it, that way the car stays on the road, after all the 2.5 core up here is cheap.<P>Thanks<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Iron Duke Wellingsley||05-16-2001||02:58 AM||||I am building my Duke right now.  I used the cam that Vader is recommending along with Hyperutectic 0.020" overbore pistons.<P>Since my car is an 84 automatic, I did not go for the porting work on the head.  Porting can give horsepower gains at the expense of torque.<P>Instead I just got a 3 angle vavle job.  I am using the Holley TBI and dominator manifold so no intake mods will be needed.  The valve springs, lifters, and rockers are all new.  In a few months, I may upgrade to the Hooker Tri Y header if I can find one.  We'll see.<P>I will dyno the car in June.  After that I will post before and after results.  ||||reg||6
Z||000004||erikred||05-16-2001||12:00 PM||||If you have the time, building the engine to 130 HP should not cost much more than a stock rebuild. You can use all stock componets in the block. Just make sure they are inspected. If you do not plan on taking this thing to the race track all the time it should hold up as well as the original motor did. Do not bother hunting down cranks from other 151 engines the are about the same strength wise. The stock rods are weak but should hold up to 130 HP. I would change to a metal rather than plastic timming gear though.<P>For several hundred more you could go for 150-200 HP by using SB Chevy, Chevy 153ci, and Mercruiser parts.||||reg||14
Z||000005||Brucepts||05-16-2001||12:12 PM||||Porting work if done correctly and checked on a flowbench will not affect torque. If you port a head/intake and concentrate on maximizing flow velocity and add cfm's without losing port velocity then you will increase the torque the motor puts out. If you just make a port "bigger" then yes you will have some problems at low rpm's which is what this motor will run at. Big ports need lots of rpm's!! Bigger isn't always better  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> If and when I get a chance I plan on doing some flowbench work on one of these heads/intakes in the very near future. My SE needs a new engine anyways, one advantage to having your own flowbench  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
